## 2. Power Steering Failures

A lot of Ford drivers, especially those with the Ford Fusion and Explorer, have run into some trouble with their power steering. One minute you’re driving smoothly, and the next, you’re struggling to turn the wheel like it’s heavy gym equipment.

Losing power steering suddenly can make controlling your vehicle much harder. It can also lead to [accidents](/blog/what-are-common-causes-of-car-accidents-in-los-angeles) if you’re driving at high speeds and lose control.

## 3. Faulty Air Conditioning

There’s nothing worse than a car AC that quits on you during a hot day. Several Ford models have had their fair share of AC troubles, where the system either blows hot air or stops working altogether. The Ford Edge SE model, for example, has experienced several issues with its cooling system when operating at high temperatures.

Usually, these cooling mishaps stem from problems like a busted compressor or a refrigerant leak. This means the AC can’t properly circulate cold air, leaving you sweating through your commute.

## 4. Engine Stalling

Imagine cruising along the road and your engine shuts down out of nowhere. It’s as scary as it sounds, and it has happened to quite a few Ford drivers.

Models like the Ford Escape and Ford EcoSport have been notorious for sudden engine stalling. The culprit? Often, it’s faulty engine parts or glitches in the car’s software.

Engine stalling is more than terrifying; it can put you and other drivers at risk of a serious accident. Always keep an eye on any recall notices or software updates from Ford to help prevent this issue from happening to you.

## 5. Swollen Lug Nuts

While it’s not the first Ford car defect you’d think of, swollen lug nuts are a surprisingly common headache.

For many Ford cars, the lug nuts are covered with a metal cap that can expand when it gets moist. As a result, when you try to use a lug wrench, it does not fit right. This swelling can make a routine tire change frustrating and time-consuming.

If you’re stuck on the side of the road with a flat and can’t get the lug nuts off, you’re in for a bit of a struggle. It’s a good idea to check these caps regularly and consider replacing them with more durable options.

## 6. Exhaust Fumes in Cabin

There have been reports, especially from Ford Explorer owners, of exhaust fumes leaking into the cabin. Breathing these in can lead to [carbon monoxide poisoning](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430740/), with symptoms like dizziness, nausea, loss of consciousness, and, in severe cases, death.

Any time you start feeling nauseous or generally unwell in the car (and you know it’s not from something else), pull over and get some fresh air immediately. Try to keep your windows open for as much of the ride as possible. Most importantly, if the vehicle is still covered under the warranty, report this issue to the manufacturer immediately.

## 7. Electric System Glitches

Electrical problems in Ford cars can range from infotainment screens going blank to batteries draining unexpectedly. These issues are often due to outdated software or faulty components that need replacing.

Either way, you’ll want to address them with the manufacturer sooner rather than later to keep your Ford running smoothly.

## 8. Fuel Pump Failures

Faulty fuel systems are another common defect in Ford vehicles. When the fuel pump isn’t working right, your car can lose power, start to sputter, experience a fuel leakage, or even refuse to start altogether.

This issue affects the vehicle’s performance and pose significant safety risks. For instance, a sudden loss of power while driving can lead to dangerous situations, especially on highways. Additionally, fuel leakage increases the risk of fire hazards, putting the driver and passengers at severe risk. If you’re facing any issues with the fuel pump under warranty, get in touch with the manufacturer as soon as possible.

## 9. Door Latch Problems

Several Ford models have been recalled due to door latch problems. With this [Ford car defect](/blog/ford-car-lemon-law), your doors might not close properly, or even swing open while you’re driving.

In addition to the safety risk, a door that doesn’t latch properly can compromise the car’s security, making it easier for theft or unauthorized access. Ensuring that any recalls are addressed promptly and checking door latches regularly can help prevent these potential hazards.

## What Can You Do?

If you own a Ford or are thinking about buying one, here are a few tips to keep in mind.

- **Stay Informed:** Keep up with recalls and service bulletins for your specific model. The [National Highway Traffic Safety Administration’s (NHTSA)](https://www.nhtsa.gov/) website is a great resource for this.

- **Regular Maintenance:** Regular check-ups by a qualified mechanic can catch many of these issues before they become serious problems.

- **Warranty and Insurance:** Make sure you understand what your warranty covers and consider extended warranty options for older cars.

- **Voice Your Concerns:** If you experience any of these Ford car defects, report them to your dealer and Ford directly. Sometimes, if enough people report a problem, a recall notice will be issued.

